```{r, include = FALSE}
knitr::opts_chunk$set(
  collapse = TRUE,
  comment = "#>",
  fig.width = 7,
  fig.height = 4.5,
  fig.path = "stanr-"
)
set.seed(42)
```

_EpiNow2_ fits its models through a choice of Stan backend.
The `rstan` backend uses models compiled when the package is installed and the `cmdstanr` backend compiles a model the first time it is used.
The `stanr` and `stanli` backends both use [stanr](https://github.com/andrjohns/stanr), which offers two ways of preparing a model and three samplers.

The `stanr` backend compiles the model to a shared library, as `cmdstanr` does.
The `stanli` backend interprets it over precompiled `stan-math` kernels instead, so fitting needs no C++ toolchain and preparing a model takes milliseconds.
Either can sample with Stan's NUTS, with static HMC, or with [WALNUTS](https://github.com/flatironinstitute/walnutpie), which adapts its step size within each trajectory.

This vignette shows how to use them and compares them against the existing backends.

# Installation

`stanr` is not on CRAN.

```{r, eval = FALSE}
install.packages(
  "stanr",
  repos = c("https://andrjohns.r-universe.dev", "https://cloud.r-project.org")
)
```

```{r setup}
library("EpiNow2")
library("ggplot2")
library("data.table")
```

# Using the backends

Select a backend, and optionally a sampler, through `stan_opts()`.

```{r, eval = FALSE}
estimate_infections(
  data = example_confirmed[1:60],
  generation_time = gt_opts(example_generation_time),
  delays = delay_opts(example_incubation_period + example_reporting_delay),
  stan = stan_opts(backend = "stanli", engine = "walnuts", cores = 4)
)
```

Both backends run their chains in parallel within a single call, so `cores` sets how many run at once.

# Comparing the backends

The rest of this vignette fits the same model with each backend and compares the time taken and the estimates produced.

```{r shared-settings}
reported_cases <- example_confirmed[1:60]
generation_time <- gt_opts(example_generation_time)
delays <- delay_opts(example_incubation_period + example_reporting_delay)
```

## Preparing the model

The first cost a user meets is getting a model ready to sample.
The `rstan` model was compiled when the package was installed, so it is already available.

```{r model-prep}
prep <- data.table(
  backend = c("cmdstanr", "stanr", "stanli"),
  elapsed = c(
    system.time(epinow2_cmdstan_model("estimate_infections"))[["elapsed"]],
    system.time(
      epinow2_stanr_model("estimate_infections", backend = "compiled")
    )[["elapsed"]],
    system.time(
      epinow2_stanr_model("estimate_infections", backend = "stanli")
    )[["elapsed"]]
  )
)
prep[]
```

The compiling backends cache their shared library, so only the first call pays this cost.
The `stanli` figure is the cost every time.

## Fitting

```{r fit-backends}
runs <- list(
  list(label = "rstan", backend = "rstan", engine = "nuts"),
  list(label = "cmdstanr", backend = "cmdstanr", engine = "nuts"),
  list(label = "stanr", backend = "stanr", engine = "nuts"),
  list(label = "stanli", backend = "stanli", engine = "nuts"),
  list(label = "stanli (walnuts)", backend = "stanli", engine = "walnuts")
)

fit_run <- function(run) {
  opts <- list(
    backend = run$backend, chains = 4, cores = 4,
    samples = 1000, warmup = 500, seed = 20240101
  )
  if (run$backend %in% c("stanr", "stanli")) {
    opts$engine <- run$engine
  }
  timing <- system.time(
    fit <- suppressMessages(estimate_infections(
      data = reported_cases,
      generation_time = generation_time,
      delays = delays,
      stan = do.call(stan_opts, opts),
      verbose = FALSE
    ))
  )
  list(fit = fit, elapsed = timing[["elapsed"]])
}

results <- lapply(runs, fit_run)
names(results) <- vapply(runs, function(x) x$label, character(1))
```

```{r timings}
timings <- data.table(
  run = names(results),
  elapsed = vapply(results, function(x) x$elapsed, numeric(1))
)
timings[, relative := elapsed / min(elapsed)][]
```

## Comparing estimates

Each run samples from the same posterior, so the estimates should agree up to Monte Carlo error.

```{r extract-rt}
rt <- rbindlist(lapply(names(results), function(label) {
  out <- as.data.table(
    summary(results[[label]]$fit, type = "parameters", params = "R")
  )
  out[, run := label][]
}))
```

```{r plot-rt}
ggplot(rt, aes(x = date, colour = run, fill = run)) +
  geom_ribbon(aes(ymin = lower_90, ymax = upper_90), alpha = 0.1,
              colour = NA) +
  geom_line(aes(y = median), linewidth = 0.8) +
  geom_hline(yintercept = 1, linetype = "dashed", colour = "grey40") +
  scale_colour_brewer(palette = "Dark2") +
  scale_fill_brewer(palette = "Dark2") +
  labs(x = "Date", y = "Effective reproduction number",
       subtitle = "Median and 90% credible interval") +
  theme_bw() +
  theme(legend.position = "bottom")
```

The estimates lie on top of one another.
Comparing the medians against the `rstan` fit puts a number on what remains.

```{r rt-differences}
wide <- dcast(rt, date ~ run, value.var = "median")
wide[, lapply(.SD, function(x) max(abs(x - rstan))),
     .SDcols = setdiff(names(wide), c("date", "rstan"))]
```

The scalar parameters are easier to read from a table than a plot.

```{r compare-params}
params <- rbindlist(lapply(names(results), function(label) {
  out <- as.data.table(summary(results[[label]]$fit, type = "parameters"))
  out[is.na(date), .(variable, median)][, run := label][]
}))

dcast(params, variable ~ run, value.var = "median")
```

# Limitations

Both backends support `method = "sampling"` only.
Use the `cmdstanr` backend for variational inference, the Laplace approximation or pathfinder.

Neither supports running chains through `{future}`, because they run them in parallel themselves.

WALNUTS adapts a diagonal metric with adaptation engaged, so it cannot be combined with fixed parameter sampling.
The simulation functions therefore use NUTS whichever engine is set.
